                {
                  "_key": "k90",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "k91",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "No rebate applies because taxable income is above ₹12 lakh.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "markDefs": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "k92",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k93",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Priya's ₹6,17,300 of exemptions and deductions under the old regime sounds like a lot. It was not enough, because the new regime's lower slab rates and higher standard deduction are worth more to her.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k94",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k95",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "How Much Would She Need to Deduct?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k96",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k97",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "For any salary, you can find the break-even point: the total old-regime exemptions and deductions at which both regimes give the same tax. For Priya, the new regime tax before cess is ₹1,45,000. Old regime tax on income above ₹10 lakh is ₹1,12,500 plus 30% of the excess, so:",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "1{,}12{,}500 + 0.30 \\times (T - 10{,}00{,}000) = 1{,}45{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; T \\approx 11{,}08{,}333",
              "caption": "T is the old-regime taxable income that gives the same tax as the new regime",
              "_key": "k98"
            },
            {
              "_type": "keyFact",
              "label": "Priya's break-even",
              "value": "About ₹6,91,667 of total deductions",
              "context": "₹18,00,000 minus ₹11,08,333. She had ₹6,17,300, about ₹74,367 short, so the new regime wins for her.",
              "_key": "k99"
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "Break-even figures are specific to one salary. Run both regimes on your own numbers every year, because your rent, investments, salary and the Budget all change. The income tax calculator on the e-filing portal does this in a few minutes.",
              "_key": "k100"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k101",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k102",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The New Regime Rebate and Marginal Relief",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k103",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k104",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Under the new regime, the rebate under section 156 of the Income-tax Act, 2025 (section 87A of the 1961 Act) wipes out tax of up to ₹60,000 when taxable income is ₹12 lakh or less. Add the ₹75,000 standard deduction under section 19 (section 16(ia)), and a salaried employee with gross salary up to ₹12,75,000 and no other income pays no tax at all. Form 16 will show the rebate in row 14 and net tax payable of zero.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k105",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k106",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Just above ₹12 lakh, ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k108",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "marginal relief",
                  "marks": [
                    "k107"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k109",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " prevents a cliff. Your tax cannot be more than the amount by which your taxable income exceeds ₹12 lakh. The relief stops mattering at roughly ₹12,70,600 of taxable income, where normal slab tax becomes lower.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "k107",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "A limit that stops tax from exceeding the income earned above a threshold. Under the new regime, tax cannot exceed the amount by which taxable income crosses ₹12 lakh."
                }
              ]
            },
